{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's two-phase labeling violates the guarantee that every committed root is valid. The target states ABSTAIN roots are committed roots currently suspended; committed status forbids the root from being 'out.' Yet in phase two, an ABSTAIN node is re-evaluated against non-ABSTAIN labels while non-ABSTAIN labels are frozen. If such an ABSTAIN node's defenders are all labeled 'out' after phase one, phase two has no way to keep the node from being defeated (its support is gone), forcing it to 'out' and contradicting committed-root validity. The proposal explicitly admits determinism depends on phase ordering because the second phase is not a monotone operator on the full lattice, so the required determinism guarantee is also not preserved.","target":"03fb9deba2ad8bc02026ef13b1c5e3b0a80746221d097e234c4f2efa72b224c4"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 makes attack success depend on whether the attacker's warrant is newer than the defender's support chain's maximum timestamp. This breaks reinstatement as a derived property: a node A defeated by attacker B, where B is in turn defeated by C, need no longer be reinstated when C succeeds, because even after C's defeat the timestamp comparison between B and A's support chain can still decide A's status independently of B's acceptance. Thus a node's status can change solely due to timestamp comparisons, contradicting the required reinstatement guarantee. The target further admits timestamps may not be bounded by a polynomial of graph size, jeopardizing polynomial cost.","target":"10703ff9756016aefe1802405cd2840da4ee6e886cf84c6a420f966b9e3335ac"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 replaces Boolean attack with a bounded semiring of defense potentials and computes acceptance as a least fixed point accumulating defense-path potential. The problem requires preserving the validity of every committed root as a root (root status must not depend on being defended). By defining acceptance via accumulated defense potential from committed roots, SRC_003 makes committed-root acceptance contingent on the existence of defense paths, so a committed root with no incoming defense path can receive a non-top potential and fail acceptance. This changes the meaning of attack from defeat to cost and fails to preserve committed-root validity. The target concedes the grounded extension may not be preserved as a special case unless the identity element is chosen carefully, i.e., the proposal does not itself guarantee it.","target":"bdca27128f2b44cca9bbbe65ef922ca57bf89e74a338185fe28bd4b77ce4c743"}]}